  "result": "The 30-minute lesson on O3 is written, with a quiz and a cheat sheet. I did not run a word count, so the length is only estimated. I judge it to fall inside the 2,700–6,750-word range for a 30-minute lesson, by my own estimate.\n\n**Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html`. It starts from your makerspace assistant and covers six parts:\n- **Context window:** what the model sees on one call, why a fuller window costs more and recalls worse (context rot), and why unrelated manual pages hurt.\n- **Between steps:** the model keeps nothing between calls, because the program re-sends the earlier steps each time. It includes a worked example counting input tokens (the numbers are illustrative).\n- **Trimming:** clearing old tool results and compacting into a summary.\n- **Between sessions:** why the assistant forgets a student's training status, and how notes and the memory tool fix it.\n- **Retrieval:** chunks, embeddings and keyword search for the long manuals, and when you may not need retrieval at all.\n- **Where each piece of information lives:** a worked example, then a faded practice task using a different student.\n\nIt has four figures, five practice checks with instant feedback, and a warm-up on the agent loop.\n\n**Quiz:** quiz 0002 is ready when you are. It has five items, and its key is `keys/0002.json`.\n\n**Also written:**\n- `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html`\n- `RESOURCES.md`, listing the three sources the lesson cites\n- `NOTES.md`, updated\n\nI read three sources: Anthropic's context-engineering article, the Claude memory tool docs and Anthropic's Contextual Retrieval post. I have not opened the links or the lesson in a browser.\n\nYou can ask me anything about the lesson in the chat. I found no vetted community for agent builders, so the \"test it with others\" step points you to your design team and advisor. The next lesson I'd suggest is writing a good tool description, which is one of your mission goals. Do you want that one, or a retake of quiz 0001 first?",
